<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=stone-tools">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stone tools</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stone tools are implements shaped from stone by knapping or grinding; the oldest known, from Lomekwi near Lake Turkana in Kenya, are dated to 3.3 million years ago, about a million years before the genus Homo. The earliest known Oldowan tools, from Nyayanga in Kenya, date to about 2.9 million years ago, and the Acheulean industry, best known for its hand axes, appears in the West Turkana area by 1.7 million years ago. Polished stone axes were important for clearing woods and forests in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and were traded over long distances.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stone-tools.jpg" alt="Stone tools" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nationalmuseumofiran · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biface_(trihedral)_Amar_Merdeg,_Mehran,_Ilam,_Lower_Paleolithic,_National_Museum_of_Iran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>3.3 million years ago</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Lomekwi, near Lake Turkana, Kenya</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Who made the oldest tools is not known: Australopithecus afarensis, Kenyanthropus platyops or a species not yet identified.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/control-of-fire">Control of fire<span>c. 1 million years ago</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/archery">Bow and arrow<span>c. 80,000 years ago</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution">Agriculture<span>c. 10,000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/trepanning">Trepanation<span>c. 6500 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=stone-tools">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
